<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Aegina</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 431 BCE (approx.) · capital Aegina</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Island state in the Saronic Gulf that minted some of the first Greek coins and was the great commercial rival of Athens until 431 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Aegina, an island between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argolid">Argolid</a>, was settled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> and had a strong <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> town at Kolonna. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> it was resettled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorians">Dorians</a> from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/epidaurus">Epidaurus</a>, and by the 7th century BCE it had become one of the busiest trading states of the Greek world.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Aegina struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> 'turtle' coins from c. 550 BCE, and its weight standard spread across much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Its merchants traded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naucratis">Naucratis</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and its oligarchic elite built the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-aphaia">Aphaia</a>, whose pediment sculptures are now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>. Aegina gave earth and water to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in 491 BCE, but its ships won the prize for valour at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-salamis">Salamis</a> in 480 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Long wars with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> ended in 457 BCE, when Aegina was forced into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a>. In 431 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> expelled the Aeginetans and settled colonists; <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sparta">Sparta</a> gave the exiles land at Thyrea. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysander">Lysander</a> restored survivors in 405 BCE, but the island never regained its former power.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/aegina.jpg" alt="Aegina" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Xocolatl 20:16, 10 April 2008 (UTC) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C3%84gina_Hafenblick.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 BCE – 431 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aegina</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion; cult of Aphaia</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Aigina</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-666&amp;focus=aegina" aria-label="Aegina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5265 820 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Aegina lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5585 1076L5591 1068M5585 1076L5591 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5591 1068h0M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5585 1076h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5585 1076h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Aegina. <a href="/atlas?year=-666&amp;focus=aegina">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

